---
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Ambiguous = `The garage is free for a short stop, then charges for
the first hour, then less for each hour after that.`;

const Questions = `Questions this brief does not answer

  - How short is "a short stop"?
  - Is exactly 30 minutes free, or does 30 start the charge?
  - Does a 61-minute stay pay for two hours, or one and a bit?
  - Is there a cap? (The brief mentions one elsewhere.)

MY DECISIONS
  - Free under 30 minutes. Exactly 30 is charged.
  - Partial hours round up. 61 minutes = 2 hours.`;

const pool = [
  { slug: "cc-traffic-light", title: "Traffic Light", stars: 1, after: "1.11" },
  { slug: "cc-movie-rating", title: "Movie Rating", stars: 1, after: "1.11" },
  { slug: "cc-shipping-tier", title: "Shipping Tier", stars: 2, after: "1.11" },
  { slug: "cc-birth-seasons", title: "Birth Seasons", stars: 1, after: "1.12" },
  { slug: "cc-hat-size-checker", title: "Hat Size Checker", stars: 1, after: "1.12" },
  { slug: "cc-letter-grade", title: "Letter Grade", stars: 2, after: "1.12" },
  { slug: "cc-parking-fee", title: "Parking Fee", stars: 2, after: "1.12" },
  { slug: "cc-ride-check", title: "Ride Check", stars: 2, after: "1.14" },
  { slug: "cc-password-strength", title: "Password Strength", stars: 2, after: "1.14" },
  { slug: "cc-coin-toss", title: "Coin Toss", stars: 1, after: "1.15" },
  { slug: "cc-magic-8-ball", title: "Magic 8-Ball", stars: 1, after: "1.15" },
  { slug: "cc-dice-duel", title: "Dice Duel", stars: 2, after: "1.15" },
];

const bonus = [
  { slug: "cc-bmi-calculator", title: "BMI Calculator", stars: 3, after: "1.12" },
  { slug: "cc-rock-paper-scissors", title: "Rock Paper Scissors", stars: 3, after: "1.15" },
];
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Code Challenge Set 2</h1>

        <h4 class="text-sn-theme">Complete any 5 of the 12 below.</h4>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />

        <section id="objective">
          <h2>What This Set Is For</h2>

          <p>
            Everything here is a decision. Your program takes something in,
            works out which case it falls into, and says something different
            depending on the answer. That is <b>selection</b> &mdash; the second
            of the three building blocks every algorithm is made of.
          </p>

          <p>
            Same rule as Set 1: <b>write your requirements list before you write
            any code</b>, and submit both.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="boundaries">
          <h2>This Set Is About Boundaries</h2>

          <p>
            In Set 1 the thing left undefined was usually a missing number. Here
            it is almost always a <b>boundary</b> &mdash; the exact value where
            one answer becomes another.
          </p>

          <p>
            Take this fragment of one of the briefs below. Read it and notice
            how much it does not tell you:
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Ambiguous}
            frame="code"
            lang="txt"
            title="From the Parking Fee brief"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            That is a normal way for a person to describe a rule, and it is not
            enough to write a program from. Here is what should end up in your
            requirements list:
          </p>

          <Code
            code={Questions}
            frame="code"
            lang="txt"
            title="Requirements — the questions half"
            showLineNumbers={false}
            class="mt-3"
          />

          <p class="mt-4">
            <b>There is no right answer to any of those questions.</b> There is
            only a decision you made or a decision you did not notice. A program
            that charges for exactly 30 minutes and one that does not are both
            correct; a program whose author never thought about it is the one
            that will be wrong in front of a customer.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>So test the boundary every time.</b> If the rule changes at 90,
            run 89.9, 90, and 90.1. That habit is worth more than any single
            challenge here, and it is the same habit that catches off-by-one
            errors in loops next week.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="challenges">
          <h2>The Challenges</h2>

          <p>Pick <b>any five</b>.</p>

          <ul class="list-unstyled mt-4">
            {
              pool.map((c) => (
                <li class="mb-2">
                  <i class="si-terminal text-sn-theme me-2"></i>
                  <a href={`/courses/ap-csp/unit-1/${c.slug}`}>{c.title}</a>
                  <span class="ms-2">
                    {Array.from({ length: c.stars }).map(() => (
                      <i class="si-star text-sn-green"></i>
                    ))}
                  </span>
                  <span class="text-body-secondary ms-2">
                    &mdash; after Activity {c.after}
                  </span>
                </li>
              ))
            }
          </ul>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="bonus">
          <h2>If Five Is Not Enough</h2>

          <p>
            These two are <b>extra credit</b>, worth 3 points each on top of the
            set. They are harder than anything in the pool and they are meant to
            be.
          </p>

          <ul class="list-unstyled mt-4">
            {
              bonus.map((c) => (
                <li class="mb-2">
                  <i class="si-flask text-sn-yellow me-2"></i>
                  <a href={`/courses/ap-csp/unit-1/${c.slug}`}>{c.title}</a>
                  <span class="ms-2">
                    {Array.from({ length: c.stars }).map(() => (
                      <i class="si-star text-sn-green"></i>
                    ))}
                  </span>
                  <span class="text-body-secondary ms-2">
                    &mdash; after Activity {c.after}
                  </span>
                </li>
              ))
            }
          </ul>

          <p class="mt-3">
            <b>Rock Paper Scissors is deliberately repetitive.</b> Three rounds
            means writing nearly the same thing three times, because you do not
            have loops yet. Noticing how annoying that is will make Activity
            1.16 land properly.
          </p>
        </section>

        <hr />

        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>For <b>each</b> challenge you complete, submit:</p>

          <ol>
            <li>
              <b>Your requirements list</b> &mdash; including the boundary
              questions the brief left open and the decision you made about each.
            </li>
            <li><b>Your program file.</b></li>
          </ol>

          <p>
            This set is worth <b>9 points</b>, graded on completion. Extra credit
            is on top and does not replace any of the five.
          </p>
        </section>

        <h1
          class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
        >
          Commence Challenges
        </h1>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
